			<div class="content" id="post-content-2862017">
									

<p>So, this was a lot to read, and thus, I have quite a few things to give feedback on.</p>
<p>For one, if you're going to post something that has a ton of ancillary materials, you should <strong>definitely</strong> have your links in place, and fill the articles in, one by one. I would also suggest announcing your SCP before you actually post it, that way people aren't seeing a bunch of random, and/or blanked, and/or strange articles show up with no context.</p>
<p>The biggest crit I can give this, and most over-arching is the lack of payoff for the amount of text there is here. There's a few different concepts here, none of which really warrant the amount of reading that took place.</p>
<p>First, your opener is pretty interesting. I'm a big fan of alternate universe skips, and i was looking forward to more.</p>
<p>From there though, you immediately drop in to an anomalous item document that says its not anomalous. Then it is anomalous. Then its ruining the O5 council, and nothing can stop us. Then its nukes? Then it's a K-class scenario? Then we gave the message to ourselves?</p>
<p>Maybe I'm not seeing the connections, but the actual anomaly here is the infohazard&#8230;which we sent to ourselves? Which was present in everything?</p>
<p>Basically, there's no focus. The narrative is unclear, and the writing, while&#8230;competent? Is mostly repetition and a lot of work for little payoff.</p>
<p>I also found the &quot;note at the end&quot; of the whole thing didn't really amount to much. From what I can gather, the infohazard forced the O5 council to release the skips as it were, yeah?</p>
<p>Either way. There was a LOT of work here, for not much payoff. I feel this could have been condensed in to a few sections of a shorter article, and had significantly better pacing.</p>
<p>Downvote in its current form (all six pages).</p>

